**Sorting a map.** A `HashMap` has no order, so you sort a view of its entries.

```java
// by key: use a TreeMap
Map<String,Integer> byKey = new TreeMap<>(map);

// by value, descending (Java 8)
List<Map.Entry<String,Integer>> list = new ArrayList<>(map.entrySet());
list.sort(Map.Entry.<String,Integer>comparingByValue().reversed());
Map<String,Integer> sorted = new LinkedHashMap<>();
for (var e : list) sorted.put(e.getKey(), e.getValue());
```

Use a `LinkedHashMap` to preserve the sorted order; a stream with `sorted(...)` and `Collectors.toMap(..., LinkedHashMap::new)` does the same.

**Singleton.** One instance, global access. Thread-safe and simple options:

```java
public enum Registry { INSTANCE; /* fields and methods */ }   // safest: serialization and reflection safe

public final class Config {
    private Config() {}
    private static class Holder { static final Config I = new Config(); }
    public static Config getInstance() { return Holder.I; }   // lazy, thread-safe via class loading
}
```

Double-checked locking also works but the field must be `volatile`. Mention that singletons make testing harder.

**Comparable vs Comparator.** `Comparable<T>` defines the class's single **natural order** inside the class (`compareTo`). `Comparator<T>` is an external, interchangeable strategy (`compare(a, b)`), so you can have many orderings without changing the class: `Comparator.comparing(Person::getName).thenComparing(Person::getAge)`.

**Java 7 features:** try-with-resources, multi-catch, diamond operator `<>`, strings in `switch`, underscores in numeric literals, NIO.2. **Java 8:** lambdas, functional interfaces, the Stream API, `Optional`, default and static interface methods, method references, the new `java.time` date-time API.

**try-with-resources.** Resources implementing `AutoCloseable` declared in the parentheses are closed automatically, in reverse order, even on exceptions; suppressed exceptions are kept. No `finally` boilerplate:

```java
try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(path))) { ... }
```

**Multi-catch.** `catch (IOException | SQLException e)` handles several unrelated exception types in one block; the types must not be subclasses of one another, and `e` is effectively final.
